.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt MOVE_PAGES 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm move_pages ,
.Nm migrate_pages
.Nd move the pages of a process between memory nodes, on a machine of one node
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In numaif.h
.Ft long
.Fn move_pages "int pid" "unsigned long count" "void **pages" "const int *nodes" "int *status" "int flags"
.Ft long
.Fn migrate_pages "int pid" "unsigned long maxnode" "const unsigned long *old_nodes" "const unsigned long *new_nodes"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Both calls answer as Linux 6.12 answers on a machine with one memory node,
node 0, where every page is on the node it could be moved to.
.Fa pid
names the process, 0 the caller's.
Another process must be one the caller may inspect as
.Xr ptrace 2
would let it read: a privileged caller may inspect any, and another caller
one whose real, effective and saved user and group IDs are the caller's
real ones, which is dumpable and not privileged.
.Pp
.Fn move_pages
takes
.Fa count
addresses in
.Fa pages
and writes one int for each to
.Fa status .
With a null
.Fa nodes
it reports where each page is: 0, its node, for a page of memory;
.Er \-EFAULT
for an address in no mapping, for a page that was only read, which is
Linux's zero page; and
.Er \-ENOENT
for an absent page of any mapping or for device memory.
With
.Fa nodes ,
an int for each page, it moves each page to its node, which must be 0, and
writes the same statuses, except that device memory is
.Er \-EFAULT .
A page of memory is on its node already.
The statuses of the pages before a failure are written.
.Fa flags
may hold
.Dv MPOL_MF_MOVE
.Pq 2
and
.Dv MPOL_MF_MOVE_ALL
.Pq 4 ,
the second for a privileged caller.
.Pp
.Fn migrate_pages
moves the pages of the process that are on a node of
.Fa old_nodes
to the nodes of
.Fa new_nodes ,
masks of
.Fa maxnode No \- 1
bits read as
.Xr set_mempolicy 2
reads its mask.
.Fa new_nodes
must name node 0.
It returns the number of pages it could not move, which is 0: the one
node a page can be on is the one it would go to.
.Ss System call interface
.Fn move_pages
is number 317 on i386 and 279 on x86-64, and
.Fn migrate_pages
294 and 256.
On the x86-64 kernel a 32-bit program's
.Fa pages
are 32-bit pointers and its masks are of 32-bit longs.
Linux 6.12 reads the wrong addresses for such a program that asks about
more than 16 pages with a null
.Fa nodes ;
this kernel reads each one from its place.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn move_pages
returns 0, and
.Fn migrate_pages
the number of pages not moved.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/numaprobe.c
asks for the status of a written page, a page only read, a page not there
and an address in no mapping, of more than 16 pages at once, and of the
pages of a process it may and may not inspect.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa pages ,
.Fa nodes ,
.Fa old_nodes
or
.Fa new_nodes
cannot be read, or
.Fa status
cannot be written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
has a bit other than the two;
the process has no address space;
a mask is one
.Xr set_mempolicy 2
refuses; or
.Fa new_nodes
does not name node 0.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
An entry of
.Fa nodes
is not 0.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Dv MPOL_MF_MOVE_ALL
from a caller without privilege; a process the caller may not inspect; or
.Fa new_nodes
names a node other than node 0 and the caller is not privileged.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process has the ID
.Fa pid .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr get_mempolicy 2 ,
.Xr mbind 2 ,
.Xr set_mempolicy 2
